import type { CancerInput, TermInput } from "@/lib/schema";
import type { Spike, SpikeSupplement } from "./index";

/**
 * SKIN CANCER: the family core and taxonomy layer of the skin round (25 September 2026), written to the standard of
 * the prostate, breast, lung, colorectal, pancreatic, triple-negative and gallbladder core files. This file owns the
 * **taxonomy** of the skin family, the routing ("which page is mine?"), the what-it-is, subtype and staging layer of
 * the two keratinocyte cancers, and the glossary the rest of the round is written against. It does not touch surgery,
 * radiotherapy or drugs, the NHS pathway and prevention, or living with the disease, which the other three layers own,
 * and it does not touch melanoma, which already has depth: melanoma is referenced and routed to, never restated.
 *
 * ===================================================================================================================
 * THE TAXONOMY OF THE SKIN FAMILY (decided here, recorded in docs/CANCER-PAGES.md)
 * ===================================================================================================================
 * The rule (docs/CANCER-PAGES.md): a cancer record is a WHO or PDQ tumour entity and carries a `parent`; a treatment
 * setting is never a record; a growth pattern or a grade is never a record. Skin is unusual in two ways that decide
 * every call below. First, the commonest cancers in the family are the ones the registries count worst, so the
 * taxonomy has to carry a counting caveat rather than a number. Second, the boundary between "cancer" and "not yet
 * cancer" is where most readers actually arrive, because actinic keratoses and Bowen's disease are far commoner than
 * the invasive cancers they precede.
 *
 *   1. `skin-cancer` IS THE ORGAN FAMILY PAGE, AND ITS FOUR CHILDREN ALREADY EXIST. `melanoma`,
 *      `basal-cell-carcinoma`, `cutaneous-scc` and `merkel-cell-carcinoma` carry `parent: "skin-cancer"` through
 *      `../cancer-subtypes.ts`. The shape matches the classification the English registry actually uses: the NDRS
 *      "Get Data Out" skin classification splits registered skin tumours into skin cancer, melanoma in situ,
 *      extramammary Paget disease and tumours of uncertain malignant potential; splits skin cancer into keratinocyte
 *      cancer, melanoma and rare cancers; and splits keratinocyte cancer into basal cell carcinoma and cutaneous
 *      squamous cell carcinoma (van Bodegraven 2023). The corpus's family page is that tree. Kaposi sarcoma,
 *      dermatofibrosarcoma protuberans and the cutaneous lymphomas arise in the skin but not from keratinocytes or
 *      melanocytes; they keep their existing parents (`sarcoma`, `non-hodgkin-lymphoma`) and are reached from the
 *      family page through `related`, exactly as the lung family reaches mesothelioma. The family page gains a
 *      GLOBOCAN mapping (sites 16 and 17 summed, melanoma of skin plus non-melanoma skin cancer) so the burden rolls
 *      up, with the counting caveat attached to it.
 *
 *   2. THE GROWTH PATTERNS AND GRADES OF THE KERATINOCYTE CANCERS GENERATE NO RECORDS, AND THE UK DATASET SAYS WHY.
 *      It would be easy to write pages for nodular, superficial and morphoeic basal cell carcinoma, and wrong. WHO
 *      names ten basal cell carcinoma subtypes in its fourth edition and nine in its fifth, but the Royal College of
 *      Pathologists dataset in force (G123, version 4, February 2019) states in terms that "there is
 *      no clinical value, with regard to management or treatment, in distinguishing between high-risk infiltrating,
 *      sclerosing and micronodular variants", that they "often co-exist in the same tumour with overlapping forms",
 *      and it adopts the proposal to combine all three under one generic term, infiltrative basal cell carcinoma.
 *      Most basal cell carcinomas contain both low-risk and high-risk patterns at once ("composite basal cell
 *      carcinoma"), and the dataset's rule is that the risk status is read from the highest-risk pattern present,
 *      irrespective of percentage or location. A pattern that cannot be separated from its neighbour in one tumour is
 *      not an entity. The same holds for the squamous side: acantholytic, desmoplastic, spindle cell and
 *      adenosquamous carcinoma are high-risk subtypes in the UK dataset, but they are reported as a subtype line on
 *      one cancer's report, not as different diseases, and the grade (well, moderately, poorly differentiated) is a
 *      grade. All of them are written here as glossary terms and as strings on the parent, which is what a reader
 *      holding a report needs.
 *
 *   3. THE PRE-CANCERS SPLIT THREE WAYS, AND THE LINE IS THE BEHAVIOUR CODE, NOT THE WHO CHAPTER. This is the
 *      judgement the round asked for, and the reason it cannot be settled by asking WHO is that WHO's own answer has
 *      moved twice. The **fifth edition of the skin Blue Book appeared in 2025** (Skin tumours, volume 12), replacing
 *      the fourth edition of 2018, and it rearranged exactly these three lesions: it moved squamous cell carcinoma in
 *      situ (Bowen disease) out of the carcinomas section and in beside actinic keratosis under "carcinoma precursors
 *      and benign simulants", and it pulled keratoacanthoma back out of squamous cell carcinoma, where the fourth
 *      edition had put it, to stand as an entity of its own. A taxonomy that followed the chapter headings would have
 *      had to move these pages twice in seven years. What has not moved is the behaviour code and the registration
 *      status, so that is the line the corpus draws: a record needs a settled behaviour of in situ or malignant and a
 *      place in the neoplasm chapter that registries actually register.
 *        **Bowen's disease is a record.** ICD-O-3.2 codes it 8081/2, in situ, and ICD-10 codes it D04, carcinoma in
 *        situ of skin, in chapter II, the neoplasm chapter. The ONS states that all in-situ neoplasms D00 to D09 are
 *        registered. It is pTis in the UICC system UK reports use, it has a national guideline of its own (BAD, Sharma
 *        2022), and it is the whole of a person's diagnosis. The corpus already gives pages to in-situ carcinomas where
 *        the same is true (`ductal-carcinoma-in-situ`, `lobular-carcinoma-in-situ`, `anal-hsil-precursor`,
 *        `germ-cell-neoplasia-in-situ`, `gallbladder-carcinoma-in-situ-and-dysplasia`). The WHO fifth edition's move
 *        is recorded on the page as a tension rather than hidden.
 *        **Actinic keratosis is a term.** ICD-O-3.2 codes it 8070/0, benign, and deliberately not 8070/2, which exists
 *        and means squamous cell carcinoma in situ. ICD-10 codes it L57.0, in chapter XII, diseases of the skin, under
 *        skin changes due to chronic exposure to nonionizing radiation: not a neoplasm code at all. No UK registry
 *        registers it. Both WHO editions file it under precursors. Treating it as a cancer page would tell several
 *        million British people they have cancer, which is neither true nor kind.
 *        **Keratoacanthoma is a term**, which is the call wave 4 already made, and the fifth edition does not overturn
 *        it. Its behaviour is the one thing nobody agrees on: ICD-O-3.2 carries it as a related term under 8071/3,
 *        malignant, while the RCPath dataset codes it SNOMED M7286/0, benign, and keeps keratoacanthoma-like squamous
 *        cell carcinoma separately at M8071/3. The RCPath states that debate continues over whether it is benign or
 *        malignant and that "no one single criterion can make a reliable distinction between squamous cell carcinoma
 *        and keratoacanthoma" (G124); the fifth edition agrees that reliable distinction is not always possible and
 *        offers the label "squamoproliferative tumour of uncertain malignant potential" for when it is not. A lesion
 *        whose behaviour code is disputed between the two systems a British report uses fails the test above, and a
 *        reader who meets the word needs the squamous cell carcinoma page, which is where its management is written.
 *
 *   4. STAGING IS TWO DIFFERENT PROBLEMS AND THE BRITISH ANSWER IS NOT THE AMERICAN ONE. Basal cell carcinoma is
 *      almost never staged in practice, and Cancer Research UK says so plainly. But the reason is not that no system
 *      covers it: in Britain it IS covered. AJCC 8's cutaneous carcinoma chapter is head and neck only and "has no
 *      staging system for cSCC on the remainder of the body", whereas UICC TNM has both a head-and-neck chapter and a
 *      carcinoma-of-the-skin chapter for the trunk and limbs, and both include basal cell, squamous cell and adnexal
 *      carcinoma. The RCPath assessed both and chose UICC "based on the staging of NMSC", noting that "UICC TNM is
 *      the version favoured by NCRAS in the UK" (G123). So a British basal cell carcinoma has a pT category on its
 *      report and an American one does not, and neither patient is ever told a stage, because the stage almost never
 *      changes what happens. For squamous cell carcinoma there are genuinely competing systems and the corpus
 *      presents neither as the answer: UICC TNM 9 (the UK's, in force from 2025), AJCC 8 (head and neck only) and the
 *      Brigham and Women's Hospital alternative, which was built precisely because the anatomical systems put almost
 *      everyone in one category. The numbers are given for each and the disagreement is stated, including the
 *      RCPath's own refusal of the Baum risk bands built on BWH. Two staging terms and one risk-feature term carry
 *      this; no record is created for a stage.
 *
 *   5. THE COUNTING PROBLEM IS PART OF THE TAXONOMY, NOT A FOOTNOTE. Every incidence figure on these pages rests on a
 *      counting rule, and the rule changed. In 1999 the UK and Ireland Association of Cancer Registries ruled that
 *      only the first basal cell carcinoma and the first squamous cell carcinoma in a person's lifetime would be
 *      registered. England now publishes on a first-per-person-per-annum basis instead, one of each per person per
 *      year, which found 67 percent more basal cell carcinomas and 42 percent more squamous cell carcinomas over
 *      2013 to 2022 than the old method (Mistry 2026), and still undercounts by about 14 basal cell carcinomas per
 *      100 patients (Kwiatkowska 2021). Scotland collects only the first basal cell carcinoma per person but
 *      registers every squamous cell carcinoma; Wales moved to the annual method from 2016; Northern Ireland
 *      publishes a figure well below the annual-method estimate. And the headline "cancer in the UK" number excludes
 *      all of it: ONS defines all cancers as ICD-10 C00 to C97 **excluding C44**, on the stated ground that
 *      non-melanoma skin cancer "is greatly under-registered". One glossary term carries this and every figure on
 *      these three pages links to it.
 *
 * WHAT ALREADY EXISTS AND IS LINKED RATHER THAN REWRITTEN. `../skin-subtypes.ts` owns the melanoma subtype pages and
 * the two advanced-disease pages (`advanced-cutaneous-scc`, `locally-advanced-bcc`), which are treatment settings
 * kept for their URLs under rule 2 of docs/CANCER-PAGES.md and not added to here. `./melanoma.ts` owns melanoma.
 * `./gap-cancers.ts` owns the three keratinocyte and Merkel records that this file patches. The glossary already
 * holds `mohs-surgery`, `carcinoma-in-situ`, `squamous-cell-carcinoma`, `perineural-invasion`, `field-cancerisation`,
 * `resection-margins`, `wide-local-excision` and `dysplasia`; those are supplemented, not duplicated.
 *
 * ONE CAUTION ABOUT THE UK SOURCES, WHICH THE PAGES STATE RATHER THAN HIDE. The two documents that decide how a
 * British skin cancer is reported, RCPath G123 and G124, are both version 4 of February 2019. Each names February
 * 2022 as its date for full revision, both are listed on the RCPath site as currently on hold, and both were written
 * against the WHO volume that preceded the 2018 fourth edition and before the 2020 and 2021 BAD guidelines. Their
 * appendices are being kept current instead: the UICC TNM 9 staging appendices were published in November 2025 and
 * the SNOMED appendices updated in February 2026. So the UK reporting standard in force in 2026 is a 2019 document
 * with 2025 and 2026 appendices, and where it and the newer guidelines differ, both are given here.
 *
 *   records patched      skin-cancer (routing, what it is, basics, staging, outlook, taxonomy notes),
 *                        basal-cell-carcinoma (what it is, subtypes, basics, staging, outlook, counting),
 *                        cutaneous-scc (what it is, subtypes, grade, basics, staging, outlook, counting),
 *                        merkel-cell-carcinoma (vocabulary and staging routing only)
 *   records created      bowens-disease (squamous cell carcinoma in situ, parent cutaneous-scc)
 *   terms                keratinocyte-cancer, keratinocyte-cancer-counting, bcc-growth-pattern,
 *                        cscc-subtype-and-grade, tnm-skin-carcinoma, bwh-staging-cscc,
 *                        skin-cancer-high-risk-features, actinic-keratosis, keratoacanthoma,
 *                        inherited-skin-cancer-syndromes
 *   supplements          carcinoma-in-situ, squamous-cell-carcinoma, perineural-invasion, field-cancerisation
 *   side data edited     ../globocan-map.ts (skin-cancer gains sites 16 and 17 with the counting caveat),
 *                        ../organ-schematics.ts (bowens-disease joins the skin drawing),
 *                        ../staging.ts (skin-carcinoma-tnm9 and cscc-bwh tables)
 *
 * Every figure is quoted from the page or document named beside it: the RCPath datasets G123 and G124 and the UICC
 * TNM 9 appendix read from the published PDFs, the ONS quality report and bulletin read from their pages, Public
 * Health Scotland and Public Health Wales read from their publications, the BWH and AJCC comparisons read through
 * their abstracts on Europe PMC, Cancer Research UK and the NHS read from their pages. Nothing is inferred and
 * nothing is recalled.
 */
